\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 1. Where this session sits\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Quick recap before the new material:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 LangChain family
\f1\b0 : LangChain (foundation + structure), LangGraph, Deep Agents, LangSmith. Langflow and similar tools are not official LangChain Inc. offerings, so they are out of scope.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Agent = model + harness.
\f1\b0  The model (GPT, Sonnet, etc.) is just the AI. Memory, tools, and orchestration around it make an agent.\
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl1\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	
\f2 \uc0\u9702 
\f1 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Check asked in class: 
\f3\i is Claude on the web an AI or an agent?
\f1\i0  Answer: an agent. It has memory, tool access, and a system prompt; the model is only one part.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Course targets 
\f0\b LangChain v1.0
\f1\b0  specifically. Most tutorials online cover older versions.\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Environment already set up: uv, OpenAI API key, Colab notebook, OpenRouter free models, VS Code.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Mental model used throughout: we are zooming into the 
\f0\b brain of the agent
\f1\b0 . Everything this session covers (model internals, messages, tools, structured output) is what sits inside that brain before we ever call 
\f4\fs26 create_agent
\f1\fs24 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 2. Looking inside a model object\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 When you initialise a chat model in LangChain, the object carries metadata worth inspecting:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 LangChain / LangChain-core version, provider name, app title\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\f4\fs26 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 max_input_tokens
\f1\fs24  and 
\f4\fs26 max_output_tokens
\f1\fs24 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Supported modalities (text in, text out, image out, etc.)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Why it matters:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Output token cap explains common errors.
\f1\b0  If a model caps output at 8,000 tokens and your response needs more, the call fails. Several students had hit this.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 You can check whether a model supports image output before wasting time coding for it.\
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Calls through OpenRouter show up as LangChain calls because the metadata travels with the request.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Key distinction: 
\f4\fs26 ChatOpenAI
\f1\fs24  is 
\f0\b not
\f1\b0  an OpenAI client object. It is a LangChain wrapper sitting on top of the OpenAI API. Behaviour differs from a raw OpenAI SDK call.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 3. Three ways to call a model\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 3.1 
\f5\fs30\fsmilli15210 invoke
\f0\fs28 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 The standard call. Pass a single message or a list of messages, get one 
\f4\fs26 AIMessage
\f1\fs24  back.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 3.2 
\f5\fs30\fsmilli15210 stream
\f0\fs28 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Returns an iterator that yields chunks as the model generates them.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs26 \cf0 full = None\
for chunk in model.stream("Why do parrots have colorful feathers?"):\
    print(chunk.text, end="", flush=True)\
    full = chunk if full is None else full + chunk\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 Notes from the demo:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Each chunk is an 
\f5\b\fs26 AIMessageChunk
\f1\b0\fs24 , carrying content, metadata, ID, and tool call chunks if any.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Chunks add together (
\f4\fs26 full = full + chunk
\f1\fs24 ) to reconstruct the complete 
\f4\fs26 AIMessage
\f1\fs24 .\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Roughly one output token per chunk. Chunk size is not under your control.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 No extra library needed. If the provider supports streaming, LangChain exposes it.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\f4\fs26 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 time.sleep()
\f1\fs24  inside the loop makes the token-by-token behaviour visible during a demo.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Purpose is user experience. ChatGPT feels fast because it streams instead of holding a 7-second response.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 You cannot request larger chunks.
\f1\b0  The OpenAI streaming API has no chunk-size parameter. If you want fewer, bigger updates, buffer two chunks yourself before pushing to the UI.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Human input can also arrive in chunks (voice apps). Handle it incrementally rather than waiting for a full utterance.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 invoke vs stream:
\f1\b0  pick based on the application. Long-form content generation wants stream; short transactional answers are fine with invoke.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 3.3 
\f5\fs30\fsmilli15210 batch
\f0\fs28 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Send several 
\f0\b independent
\f1\b0  requests together and process them in parallel.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs26 \cf0 responses = model.batch([q1, q2, q3])\
\
# or, to get each result as soon as it finishes:\
for response in model.batch_as_completed([q1, q2, q3]):\
    print(response)\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 batch
\f1\fs24  returns everything once the whole batch is done. 
\f4\fs26 batch_as_completed
\f1\fs24  yields results out of order, as each one finishes.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Requests must be independent of each other.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Cost clarification
\f1\b0  (raised by a student, worth remembering):\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The system message is still sent with 
\f0\b every
\f1\b0  request. Batching does not deduplicate tokens.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Savings come from two places: provider-side batch APIs (OpenAI's async Batch API runs roughly 50% cheaper), and infrastructure (1,000 requests instead of 1,000,000 means far fewer connections and round trips).\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Latency does not increase for end users if you use 
\f4\fs26 batch_as_completed
\f1\fs24 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

