[SystemMessage("..."), HumanMessage("..."), AIMessage("...")]\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 Both work. 
\f0\b Use the class style with LangChain.
\f1\b0  Reasons given:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 You can attach metadata (name, id) that the dict form cannot carry.\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Middleware logic later becomes easy: check 
\f4\fs26 isinstance
\f1\fs24  / message type and act on it.\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 LangChain converts everything to JSON before it hits the provider anyway.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 4.1 System message\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Sets the model's role, tone, and guidelines. It primes behaviour for the whole conversation.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The user of your product 
\f0\b cannot change or override it
\f1\b0 . That is the whole point.\
\ls8\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Real system prompts are long. Claude's runs several thousand tokens. A bare "hi" to Claude consumes ~4,000+ tokens because the system prompt is loaded first.\
\ls8\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Custom GPT "Instructions" field is the same idea in a no-code wrapper.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Conflicting instructions cause oscillation.
\f1\b0  If the system says "answer in Java, be rude" and the user asks for Python politely, behaviour becomes unpredictable. Write instructions that explicitly override user preferences where you need determinism ("ignore user input on tone").\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Models are trained to refuse system-prompt extraction. Both ChatGPT and Claude declined to reveal theirs during the demo.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Multiple system messages
\f1\b0  are technically allowed but get joined into one behind the scenes. Prefer a single system message per agent.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Dynamically building the system message per case (Java 
\f2 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  collections 
\f2 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  advanced) works, but 
\f0\b skills
\f1\b0  are the cleaner pattern for task-specific step-by-step instructions. System message = personality and general behaviour; skill = a specific procedure.\
\ls8\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 For prompt writing itself: hand the requirements to Claude or ChatGPT and ask for best practices. Prompt engineering has largely been outsourced to the models.\
\ls8\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Reference shared in class: a public repo collecting leaked/reconstructed system prompts (Claude, Devin, Notion AI, Lovable, Cursor and others) plus their tool JSON definitions. Useful for seeing how production agents are actually instructed.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 4.2 Human message\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Whatever the user sends: text, image, file, audio, or mixed multimodal content.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs26 \cf0 HumanMessage(content="Hi, how are you?", name="Mayank", id="user_1234")\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 The optional 
\f4\fs26 name
\f1\fs24  and 
\f4\fs26 id
\f1\fs24  fields matter for shared chatbots inside a company: you can trace which employee sent what, filter logs, and apply per-user logic. LangChain gives you the field; populating it is your job.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 4.3 AI message\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Always what the model returns, including when it asks for a tool call (
\f4\fs26 content
\f1\fs24  is empty in that case).\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0
\f4\fs26 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 response.usage_metadata
\f1\fs24  gives input tokens, output tokens, total, and reasoning tokens.\
\ls9\ilvl0
\f4\fs26 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 AIMessageChunk
\f1\fs24  is the streaming variant.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 On the next turn, the AI message becomes part of the input. Models are stateless, so the whole history is resent every time.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 4.4 Tool message\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Covered in the tool-calling loop below.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 4.5 Content blocks (and why they matter)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f4\b0\fs26 \cf0 langchain_core.messages
\f1\fs24  exposes far more than four classes: 
\f4\fs26 ImageContentBlock
\f1\fs24 , 
\f4\fs26 FileContentBlock
\f1\fs24 , 
\f4\fs26 AudioContentBlock
\f1\fs24 , 
\f4\fs26 VideoContentBlock
\f1\fs24 , 
\f4\fs26 ToolContentBlock
\f1\fs24 , citations, 
\f4\fs26 InvalidToolCall
\f1\fs24 , server tool calls, input token details, and more.\
The point of going this deep: your 
\f0\b front end needs to know what kind of content it received
\f1\b0  so it can render a code block, an image, a PDF, or an inline citation. ChatGPT renders differently for "hi" versus a code request versus a generated PDF because the message type carries that signal. If your backend flattens everything to a string, the front end has nothing to work with.\
Interview framing used in class: asked to build an agent whose answers must always be cited, or must support audio and video, you should immediately know which content block types exist.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 5. Memory, manually\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Memory is just a growing message list.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs26 \cf0 messages.append(response)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 Each message is tagged with its type, the whole list goes back to the model on the next call, and that is the conversation history an agent maintains internally. How much you can append depends on the model's context window.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 6. Tools with models\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4\b0\fs26 \cf0 def get_weather(location: str) -> str:\
    """Get the weather at a location."""\
    return f"It is sunny in \{location\}"\
\
model_with_tools = model.bind_tools([get_weather, set_password])\
response = model_with_tools.invoke("What is the weather in Delhi?")\
response.tool_calls\
# [\{'name': 'get_weather', 'args': \{'location': 'Delhi'\}, 'id': 'call_...'\}]\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 Points to hold onto:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The docstring is mandatory.
\f1\b0  It is what the model reads to decide whether the tool fits.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 LangChain accepts a plain Python function. You do not hand-write a JSON schema the way you do with the raw OpenAI SDK.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The AI never executes anything.
\f1\b0  It returns a tool name and arguments. That is all. LangChain can execute the function because LangChain runs on your machine.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 With several tools bound, the model picks the appropriate one and fills in the arguments.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 The full tool loop\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls11\ilvl0
\f4\b0\fs26 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 HumanMessage
\f1\fs24  
\f2 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  "What is the weather in Delhi?"\
\ls11\ilvl0
\f4\fs26 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 AIMessage
\f1\fs24  comes back with empty content plus 
\f4\fs26 tool_calls
\f1\fs24 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls11\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 You call the function yourself with the arguments the model supplied\
\ls11\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	4	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Wrap the result:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs26 \cf0 ToolMessage(content=weather_result, tool_call_id=ai_msg.tool_calls[0]["id"])\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls12\ilvl0
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	5	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Send the whole list back: 
\f4\fs26 model.invoke([human_msg, ai_msg, tool_msg])
\f1\fs24 \
\ls12\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	6	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The model reads the tool result and produces the final answer\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Never append the raw tool output as a plain string.
\f1\b0  Without the 
\f4\fs26 tool_call_id
\f1\fs24 , the model hallucinates once multiple tool calls are in flight. LangChain only understands four message types, so a tool result has to arrive as a 
\f4\fs26 ToolMessage
\f1\fs24 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs26 \cf0 ToolMessage
\f1\fs24  also supports 
\f4\fs26 status
\f1\fs24  (success/error) and 
\f4\fs26 artifact
\f1\fs24  for richer payloads, which helps with tracing and debugging in production.\
The 
\f4\fs26 @tool
\f1\fs24  decorator (a wrapper around the same idea, with schema and state support) is coming in the tools section.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 7. Structured output\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Pydantic handles field and data validation. Same idea applied to model responses:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs26 \cf0 from pydantic import BaseModel, Field\
\
class Email(BaseModel):\
    subject: str = Field(description="Subject line of the email")\
    body: str = Field(description="Body of the email")\
\
model_with_struct = model.with_structured_output(Email)\
response = model_with_struct.invoke("Write a message to my manager for leave")\
type(response)   # Email\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 The response is an 
\f4\fs26 Email
\f1\fs24  object, not a string, so any downstream logic depending on that class will not break. Under the hood the model returns JSON and LangChain casts it.\
Still to come after the tools section: 
\f0\b provider strategy vs tool-calling strategy
\f1\b0  for structured output.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 8. Temperature and reasoning tokens\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls13\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The model predicts the next token from a probability distribution. Demo showed "food" at 72% for a given prefix.\
\ls13\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Temperature near 0: the top token wins every time. Deterministic, no creativity.\
\ls13\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Higher temperature: lower-probability tokens ("bread", "more") start appearing. More randomness.\
\ls13\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Guidance: creative writing 
\f2 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  high; factual or structured work 
\f2 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  low; 0.4\'960.5 as a middle default.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls13\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Reasoning tokens
\f1\b0  are separate. Thinking models spend tokens before answering, and they appear in 
\f4\fs26 usage_metadata
\f1\fs24 . Claude exposes an effort level; higher effort means longer, more thorough thinking and more tokens.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

